          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "A type 2 diabetes diagnosis means your blood sugar (glucose) stays above the normal range over time. In this condition, the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a hormone that moves sugar from the blood into cells. The pancreas, which makes insulin, cannot keep up with the extra demand. It usually develops gradually over years, and many people have no symptoms when it is found. (Source: General clinical reference, *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*.)\n\nManagement is mostly ongoing self-care, with your clinician guiding it:\n\n- **Weight:** If your weight is above the healthy range, even a sustained loss of about 5% helps blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. Some people reach remission, meaning normal glucose without glucose-lowering medication. Remission is not a cure, and monitoring continues. (Source: national diabetes association nutrition guidance.)\n- **Activity:** The guidance suggests at least 150 minutes a week of moderate-to-vigorous activity, spread over at least three days, with no more than two days in a row without activity, plus strength training on two or three non-consecutive days. (Source: diabetes physical activity guidance.)\n- **Medication and follow-up:** Diabetes is largely self-managed between appointments. Structured diabetes education is described as a core part of care, and it is offered at diagnosis. (Source: diabetes self-management guidance.)\n\nMy search did not return the part of the reference that explains how diagnosis is confirmed or which blood tests are used, so I can't explain that from these sources.\n\nQuestions you might want to ask your doctor:\n\n- What test results led to this diagnosis, and what do the numbers mean for me?\n- Is there a chance this could go into remission, and what would that take?\n- What would a realistic weight or activity goal look like for me?\n- Am I being referred for diabetes education or a dietitian?\n- How often should I come in, and what should I track at home?\n- What side effects should I watch for with any medication you prescribe, and what should I do if I can't afford it or find it hard to take?\n\nPlease don't change or stop any medication without talking to your clinician first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
