          "type": "turn",
          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "For type 2 diabetes, the retrieved guidance focuses on a few practical points rather than a list of foods:\n\n- **\"Sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free.\"** Starch raises blood glucose about as much as sugar does. (Source: National diabetes association nutrition guidance, *Diet for type 2 diabetes*.)\n- **Fruit is not off-limits.** Whole fruit is part of the recommended eating patterns. (Same source.)\n- **Carbohydrate amounts are set individually.** The guidance does not give a single carbohydrate target. Your clinician or a dietitian sets that. (Same source.)\n- **Weight matters, if your weight is above the healthy range.** A sustained loss of 5% gives measurable benefit to blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. (Same source.)\n\nThe same corpus also notes that most dietary sodium (salt) comes from packaged and restaurant foods such as bread, processed meat, cheese, sauces and soups, not the salt shaker. Reading labels for sodium per serving helps more than any single swap. This comes from the blood-pressure diet guidance, which is relevant if you also have high blood pressure. (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*.)\n\nMy search did not return a full list of recommended foods, so I can't give you one from these sources.\n\nTo give more useful guidance, it would help to know:\n\n- Whether you have any other diagnoses, such as high blood pressure or high cholesterol\n- Your current weight goal, if your clinician has set one\n- Whether you have any food allergies, intolerances or cultural or dietary preferences\n- Whether you have been referred to a dietitian or diabetes education course\n\nPlease don't change any medication based on diet changes without talking to your clinician first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
